ation mechanisms  one of the things I hope you have  learned today is there are some new  vocabulary that you've picked up that  you can use that will help you express  what it is that your code is trying to  do one of the things that I do want to  offer as a caveat is that in the same  way that if you put all these features  into like one function it would be  obnoxious it would be very similar to  meeting something with a really large  vocabulary that's really hard to  understand right don't go back to the  office on Monday and be like I'm gonna  use intersection types and union types  and string literals for everything  because that's not what you want but now  that you know that these things exist  you know I hope that you'll see  situations in the near future like oh  you know what I really want to be able  to do here is I want to be able to  communicate that this is going to be  Type A or type B and I can just using  you know type for that so hopefully  there's you picked up some new  vocabulary today and it'll help you  build better more reliable software in  the future so thanks for coming out  today like it said I've got a blog post  up on my blog humble tool Smith comm  that I there's a little bit deeper dive  into error handling  all the code we looked at today other  than the code that I literally typed in  the last ten minutes is up on github at  typescript beyond the basics all of  these slides again except for the ones  that a the one I added this morning are  up on SlideShare so if you wanted any of  this information down the road you can  get it there if you want to talk about  this more  feel free to ask I'll be hanging around  for a little bit afterwards the easiest  way to find me online is on twitter at  powder Eric I love to talk to you more  about this stuff or answer any questions  you might have so thanks for coming out  this has been fun and have a great rest  of the conference  [Applause]
 

